---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
import Ramp from '../../components/Ramp.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base title="Colour" description="How ERA's colour ramps are structured and which step does which job.">
  <h1>Colour</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every ramp runs 50 to 950 and every step has a job. The mapping is identical in all three brands and both modes, which
    is what lets a component ask for a border without knowing which brand it is in.
  </p>

  <h2>What each step is for</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Steps</th><th>Job</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>50 to 200</td><td>Backgrounds and tints</td><td>The subtle fill behind a status badge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>300</td><td>Hovered tint</td><td>A selected row being hovered</td></tr>
      <tr><td>400 and 500</td><td>Borders</td><td>A field outline, a divider that needs to be seen</td></tr>
      <tr><td>600</td><td>Hovered border</td><td>A field under the pointer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>700 and 800</td><td>Solid fills</td><td>The primary button</td></tr>
      <tr><td>900 and 950</td><td>Text</td><td>Body copy, headings, the only two steps that clear AA</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <blockquote>
    900 and 950 are the only steps that carry body text. Anything lighter fails AA against a surface in both modes. That is
    not a limitation to work around, it is the structure: text hierarchy comes from size and weight, not from a third grey.
    The <a href={`${base}/accessibility`}>accessibility page</a> has the measurements.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>The brand ramp</h2>

  <p>This is the one that changes per brand. Switch brand at the top of the page and watch only this move.</p>

  <Demo label="--brand-primary-scale-{step}" padded>
    <div style="inline-size: 100%;">
      <Ramp name="Primary" prefix="--brand-primary-scale" note="Iris, Cyan or Jade depending on brand" />
    </div>
  </Demo>

  <h2>Support ramps</h2>

  <p>
    Shared across brands, on purpose. A warning should not change meaning because you switched product. Only the brand
    accent is allowed to be a brand decision.
  </p>

  <Demo padded>
    <div style="inline-size: 100%;">
      <Ramp name="Neutral" prefix="--brand-neutral" note="Surfaces, borders, text" />
      <Ramp name="Success" prefix="--brand-success" />
      <Ramp name="Danger" prefix="--brand-danger" />
      <Ramp name="Warning" prefix="--brand-warning" />
      <Ramp name="Info" prefix="--brand-info" />
    </div>
  </Demo>

  <h2>Choosing a token</h2>

  <p>Work from the job backwards, not from the colour forwards.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>You want</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The page behind everything</td><td><code>--sem-bg-canvas</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A card, panel or table row</td><td><code>--sem-bg-surface</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>An inset or well</td><td><code>--sem-bg-surface-sunken</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Body text</td><td><code>--sem-fg-primary</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Supporting copy</td><td><code>--sem-fg-secondary</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A divider inside a card</td><td><code>--sem-border-subtle</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A field or card edge</td><td><code>--sem-border-default</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The one main action on a view</td><td><code>--sem-primary-bg</code> with <code>--sem-primary-fg-on-bg</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A status in a dense list</td><td>The <code>-subtle</code> pair, never the solid one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hover on anything</td><td><code>--sem-state-hover</code>, which is an overlay and works on any surface</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Do not</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Do not reach for a primitive. <code>var(--colors-iris-700)</code> in a component is a brand decision hardcoded in the wrong place.</li>
    <li>Do not use a solid status fill for a row in a list. A page of solid reds is unreadable; that is what the subtle pair is for.</li>
    <li>Do not use colour as the only signal. Pair it with a word or a shape.</li>
    <li>Do not invent a lighter grey for "less important" text. Use a smaller step on the type scale instead.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
